  "reconstructibility": 1,
  "reason": "The records collectively indicate that high-temperature treatment, including vacuum exposure, can produce rapid element-specific outward diffusion, depletion, segregation, and preferential evaporation in multicomponent high-entropy alloys. R9 and R16 are particularly relevant because they show preferential surface-directed diffusion and bulk depletion during heating of an equiatomic FCC HEA, while R5 connects rapid heating to selective evaporation and R12 questions universal entropy-based phase stability. However, none identifies surface-energy minimization by Au and Cu in sub-10-nm AuCoCuNiPt particles, nor the coupling between nanoparticle reshaping, locally enhanced diffusion, surface segregation, and evaporation. Thus the literature points in the right direction, but selecting this nanoparticle system and proposing the specific surface-energy-driven mechanism would require a substantial new idea."
